#include <vector>

double fanout_pnl(const std::vector<double>& legs) {
    ThreadPool pool(4);
    std::vector<std::future<double>> futs;
    futs.reserve(legs.size());
    for (double leg : legs)
        futs.push_back(pool.submit([leg] { return leg; }));
    double total = 0;
    for (auto& f : futs) total += f.get();   // blocks per leg until done
    return total;
}
// fanout_pnl({120.50, -80.25, 15.75, -5.00}) == 51.00
```
**(a)** Each future is a private one-shot channel for one task; `get()` consumes only that channel, so order is irrelevant to correctness (only to when you block). **(b)** For `submit()` the future holds the packaged_task's shared state, discarding it loses the result silently. For `std::async(std::launch::async, ...)` the discarded future **blocks in its destructor** until the task finishes, silently serialising your code. The habit of keeping futures alive and getting them explicitly keeps both paths correct.
Arithmetic check: 120.50, 80.25 = 40.25; 40.25 + 15.75 = 56.00; 56.00, 5.00 = **51.00**.

